  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>The expressions \(p(200 − 5p)\) and \(-5p^2 + 200p\) define the same function. The function models the revenue a school would earn from selling raffle tickets at \(p\) dollars each.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>At what price or prices would the school collect $0 revenue from raffle sales? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>$0 and $40. For example, if the equation \(p(200−5p)=0\) is true, then either \(p\) is equal to 0, or \(200−5p\) is equal to 0. If \(200−5p=0\), then \(p\) is 40, because \(200−5(40)\) is 0.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>The school staff noticed that there are two ticket prices that would result in a revenue of $500. What equation could you use to determine what those two prices are?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter an equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>We could write \(p(200 − 5p) = 500\) and \(-5p^2 + 200p = 500\). Approximate solutions can be found using trial and error or graphing. However, we don’t yet know an exact way to solve either equation.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    
  
  
  
  </problem></exercise>
</example><!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>Can you find the following prices without graphing?</para>
    
    <para>If the school charges $10, it will collect $1500 in revenue. Find another price that would generate $1500 in revenue.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter another price.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>$30</para>
    
    <para>Moving the same distance away from each horizontal intercept, at $0 and $40, results in equal revenue. This means $10 and $30 earn an equal amount of revenue.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>If the school charges $28, it will collect $1680 in revenue. Find another price that would generate $1680 in revenue.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter another price.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>$12</para>
    
    <para>Moving the same distance away from each horizontal intercept, at $0 and $40, results in equal revenue. This means $12 and $28 earn an equal amount of revenue.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Find the price that would produce the maximum possible revenue. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the price that would produce the maximum possible revenue.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>$20. Moving the same distance away from each horizontal intercept results in equal revenue. The farther away we move from one horizontal intercept in the direction of the other intercept, the greater the revenue. An optimal ticket price of $20, producing $2000 in revenue, is found in the middle point between the two intercepts, at \(p = 20\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

